# LIVE REGRESSION FIXTURE (graduated out of `known_gaps/`) — the
# `&`-in-an-OWNING-POSITION class, costume 4 of 7: a DICT-LITERAL VALUE
# (`{1: &c.fd}`).
#
# REJECTED at `gg check` as intended. A container-literal value position is an
# ownership boundary; the map must own its value. Before the reject, `gg check`
# was clean on both backends and the sigil was silently DROPPED (printed 10).
#
# ⚠ THE CODE IT FIRES IS NOT THE CODE THIS CLASS IS OWED. The reject arrives as
# `error[E_AmpInOperandPosition]` — the OPERAND-position rule, not a dedicated
# ownership-BOUNDARY diagnostic. Correct and actionable here (a dict-literal
# value has no callee), but three siblings of this class — ctor / push / enum
# payload — are STILL ACCEPTED at HEAD because they DO have a callee. This
# fixture's green does not close the class; the parent TODO row stays open.
#
# ORACLE: this shape is outside ggdef's phase-0 subset, so it is pinned against
# the RULE rather than against a third implementation. Recorded explicitly
# rather than left implicit (Core #9). Its in-subset siblings — ctor, push, enum
# — all reject in ggdef, and a dict-literal value is the same kind of position.
#
# The `fd` seed is COMPUTED, not a literal.
#
# See `sound_amp_owning_position_ctor.gg` for the full sibling list and the
# ruling this class rests on.
struct Counter:
    int fd

int seed():
    return 10

void main():
    Counter c = Counter(seed())
    Dict[int, int] d = {1: &c.fd}
    print(d[1])
